{"title":"2023 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Calculus)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Calculus)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Trigonometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Trigonometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Continuum_Math_(Trig)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Continuum Math (Trig)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>What is the maximum area of an isosceles trapezoid that has legs of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and one base twice as long as the other?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/e/a9ea4ad1d23be1e4ff208e8159be7f444100e0c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }\\frac 54 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } \\frac 87 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)} \\frac{5\\sqrt2}4 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } \\frac 32  \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } \\frac{3\\sqrt3}4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"417\" height=\"43\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the trapezoid be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/2/af2814bd31c92960f398458a1b19f4d1527668e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD = BC = 1, \\; AB = x, CD = 2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"275\" height=\"18\" />. Extend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> to meet at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Then, notice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/8/f68c97c054a35d499119e12cb4a1204ab30115d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABE \\sim \\triangle DCE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"18\" /> with side length ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/e/32e1a1a7adf986532b7598e896785a56c785f779.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1:2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/4/6e4f316ea25f269e3a101ca971347815f1796ec9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE = BE = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"117\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/3/fe38ebf79e312ce8912ddc563040b58c0f0c8b65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[DCE] = 4 \\cdot [ABE]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"156\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/3/7c347fc2d4df0dea790625fd4f096abf0cd3d4cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABCD] = [DCE] - [ABE] = \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot [DCE]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"329\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>The problem reduces to maximizing the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/5/365f98311a8062205054014e404205f5028cbbf4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[DCE]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />, an isosceles triangle with legs of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Analyzing the sine area formula, this is clearly maximized when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/1/2a10a4b85e173a10e123251a6ee351c222d54e2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DEC = 90^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"109\" height=\"15\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/7/6e74bc9d261d8b70742a86292f78d41f7bf0e7bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[DCE] = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/b/5dbb59535d7b619f626a243a3b09abca189d78ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABCD] = \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot 2 = \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"183\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>-PIDay\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Denote by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> the length of the shorter base.\nThus, the height of the trapezoid is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/e/96eba3ae441680784ee12fbc66f4708309d7f789.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\sqrt{1^2 - \\left( \\frac{x}{2} \\right)^2} . \\end{align*}\" width=\"101\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the area of the trapezoid is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/5/ac51b9def7d71c06f00a3048062149379c12587b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\frac{1}{2} \\left( x + 2 x \\right) \\sqrt{1^2 - \\left( \\frac{x}{2} \\right)^2}  &amp; = \\frac{3}{4} \\sqrt{x^2 \\left( 4 - x^2 \\right)} \\\\ &amp; \\leq \\frac{3}{4} \\frac{x^2 + \\left( 4 - x^2 \\right)}{2} \\\\ &amp; = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } \\frac{3}{2}} , \\end{align*}\" width=\"326\" height=\"143\" />\n</p><p>where the inequality follows from the AM-GM inequality and it is binding if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/5/d75eb9df217af1faf5d4b2c197ec6f2d615b223b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 = 4 - x^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"93\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>~Steven Chen (Professor Chen Education Palace, www.professorchenedu.com)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Calculus.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Calculus)\">Solution 3 (Calculus)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Calculus)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Derive the expression for area\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/4/da430f83d1085d6f4c74bf0878508c08e8cd21c1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = \\frac{3}{4}x\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\]\" width=\"125\" height=\"37\" />\nas in the solution above. To find the minimum, we can take the derivative with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/3/f73a3a0b489f9f8511d54cff4852337870dae53f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{dA}{dx} = \\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{4-x^2}+\\left(\\frac{3x}{4}\\right)\\frac{-2x}{2\\sqrt{4-x^2}} = \\frac{6-3x^2}{2\\sqrt{4-x^2}}.\\]\" width=\"384\" height=\"44\" />\nThis expression is equal to zero when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/a/38a4e87ae3fdda194f2452d8ecc471b418f6226a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\pm\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"75\" height=\"21\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> has two critical points at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/8/de89706de8832f5833390d2721c8632117e5c37d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pm\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" />. But given the bounds of the problem, we can conclude <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/c/e7c316900cf6486a6acb2558d186c15974753492.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"61\" height=\"21\" /> maximizes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> (alternatively you can do first derivative test). Plugging that value back in, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/0/9503af4fc37a761e86051538fc52fbe3b7ad2108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{\\text{max}} = \\boxed{(\\text{D})\\ \\frac{3}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"122\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~cantalon\n</p><p>(Slightly Simpler)\n</p><p>Or rewrite the expression for area to be\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/8/208f7c9fa5cffebc6049321d01bec91b326feb24.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = \\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{4x^2-x^4}\\]\" width=\"132\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Now to find the minimum, we can just find the minimum of what's inside the square root (since the square root function is increasing). Take the derivative of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/7/eb79513a9d4bb4e62d618c52ff2dc182be9626d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4x^2-x^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/1/901083dd69e265836d5feb5ab07a58f7f1a217ad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f&#39;(x)=8x-4x^3.\\]\" width=\"136\" height=\"20\" />\nThis is equal to zero at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/0/a40dd14b6938474abc70d09ab92d994dd2e81b7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=0,\\pm\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"23\" /> but the solution must be positive so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/1/db1f87c7de444f61b4aa1184aeae8678599199c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Trigonometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Trigonometry)\">Solution 4 (Trigonometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Trigonometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the length of the shorter base of the trapezoid be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/c/2ec701493fa72dda5d6747fe14f30e7b1c2fb6c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> and the height of the trapezoid be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/e/7feacfc34a7a14d353e5f684a301f9241fb29610.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(100); pair A=(-1, 0), B=(1, 0), C=(0.5, 0.5), D=(-0.5, 0.5);  draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, black);  label(&quot;$2x$&quot;,(0,0.58),(0,0)); label(&quot;$2x$&quot;,(0,-0.08),(0,0)); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(-0.75,-0.08),(0,0)); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(0.75,-0.08),(0,0)); draw(D--(-0.5,0),black); draw(C--(0.5,0),black); label(&quot;$y$&quot;,(0.58,0.25)); label(&quot;$y$&quot;,(-0.42,0.25)); [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"128\" />\n</p><p>Each leg has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/d/f1dd4184b561ea747be652fc3dee7fc5e1c5103f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are positive real numbers. The general solution to this equation is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/0/a4038b37506352b9ecc871c57d73d2668aef1b4e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(x,y)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)\\]\" width=\"151\" height=\"18\" /> for any number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/32596e0fb2de211aa479a771ce6cf624188a6235.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;t&lt;\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"34\" /> so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are positive. The area to maximize is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/8/b98e99a4540a81348887c435cbfc96ab836f5501.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{2}(2x+4x)y=3xy\\]\" width=\"148\" height=\"37\" /> Hence, we maximize <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/1/9b1a2e9b4d211277a08d52d7034352e3463adaca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\sin t\\cos t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"83\" height=\"14\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/32596e0fb2de211aa479a771ce6cf624188a6235.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;t&lt;\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"34\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/d/a2d48ee43ff94894c0f3323d2f6c8a4b453c83c0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}     3xy &amp;= 3\\sin t\\cos t \\\\     &amp;= \\frac{3}{2}(2\\sin t\\cos t) \\\\     &amp;= \\frac{3}{2}\\sin(2t) \\end{align*}\" width=\"159\" height=\"104\" />\nThe maximum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/5/2b5960b59d9a44f40131f28a7eefcd4b21f09396.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(2t)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, thus the maximum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/1/3c1e2fb120953f946279e3807829929b390f8934.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/c/e8cf4da11a02fc03d67dac03f1c209cffe52d221.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\text{(D) }\\frac{3}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"57\" height=\"49\" /> which occurs at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/8/2c8f5ae60193220c3aa73154e67bdaab5863b3c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"34\" />, satisfying the inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/32596e0fb2de211aa479a771ce6cf624188a6235.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;t&lt;\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"34\" />.\n</p><p>~Robabob1\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/c/2ec701493fa72dda5d6747fe14f30e7b1c2fb6c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> as the two bases. We can straightforwardly find that the height of the trapezoid is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/d/f4d6ffe741e5ba7ceea50ec6f2eb2ca7fdc7579e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x^2}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"73\" height=\"45\" /> by the Pythagorean theorem. \n</p><p>The area of this trapezoid is then given by the expression <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/9/889dace14b1fef658d44c02219708712a56fa2dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x+2x}{2}\\cdot\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x^2}{4}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"147\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> be the maximum value that this expression achieves. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is positive and assuming <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/9/ed99c85dd00eb11d60bb8d0332530445cf25c5ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" />, we can perform the following operations to maximize <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55a42d9581ae98acac963f5d94bcb7022f3a504c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16m^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"15\" />, thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/5/0f521656db779d024e6aff0f9a3771ac28d2c3db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m^2$\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" />, and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />. We have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/0/2a0b3ee2387248a7fde29165e799ff6182b2be10.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{x+2x}{2}\\cdot\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x^2}{4}} = m\\]\" width=\"177\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/6/dc68c58767e4358965d1ab85c0f6fc9941951c93.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{9x^2}{4}\\cdot\\frac{4-x^2}{4} = m^2\\]\" width=\"141\" height=\"39\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/0/b504c7f11ad89bc78ce496a41e0ea79bbf2ab167.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[-9x^4 + 36x^2 = 16m^2.\\]\" width=\"167\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>The maximum value of this quartic occurs at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/d/08dea275023eb7777308e4cad54ef232d8516f24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 = \\frac{-36}{2(-9)} = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"128\" height=\"43\" />, or when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/d/3bdfbbdf7e603419963338921000fd77d1d316fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\sqrt{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>It follows that the area of the trapezoid is equal to \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/d/94d36050241e16900e909e03740554a31052af6d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\boxed{\\text{(D) }\\frac{3}{2}}.\\]\" width=\"160\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>-Benedict T (countmath1)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/WlXBbaHl-z4\">https://youtu.be/WlXBbaHl-z4</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Continuum_Math_.28Trig.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Continuum_Math_(Trig)\">Video Solution by Continuum Math (Trig)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Continuum Math (Trig)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/fU4EjrwBXU4\">https://youtu.be/fU4EjrwBXU4</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/e6Et9KBkRy8\">https://youtu.be/e6Et9KBkRy8</a>\n</p><p><br />\n~Steven Chen (Professor Chen Education Palace, www.professorchenedu.com)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><